name: input and exit status
intro: Real programs take input: arguments from the command line, bytes from
intro: standard input. Both arrive unchecked, so you check them.
intro:
intro:     int main(int argc, char **argv)
intro:
intro: `argc` counts the words on the command line, the program's own name
intro: included. `argv[i]` is word i as text; argv[0] is the name, so your
intro: arguments are argv[1] up to argv[argc - 1]. A program that takes no
intro: arguments keeps `int main(void)`.
intro:
intro: Turn text into a number with `strtol`, never `atoi`. atoi returns 0 for
intro: "abc" and for "0", so you cannot tell them apart, and it has no way to
intro: report overflow. strtol tells you everything:
intro:
intro:     char *end;
intro:     errno = 0;
intro:     long n = strtol(s, &end, 10);
intro:     if (end == s || *end != '\0' || errno == ERANGE)
intro:         /* bad */
intro:
intro: `end` is left pointing at the first character it could not use.
intro: `end == s` means no digits at all -- including the empty string, which
intro: would otherwise sneak through as 0. `*end != '\0'` means junk after the
intro: digits ("12abc"). errno is set to ERANGE when the number does not fit
intro: a long; zero it first, because nothing else clears it. Needs
intro: <errno.h> and <stdlib.h>.
intro:
intro: `LONG_MAX`/`LONG_MIN` come from <limits.h>. Test before adding:
intro:
intro:     if ((n > 0 && sum > LONG_MAX - n) ||
intro:         (n < 0 && sum < LONG_MIN - n))
intro:         /* would overflow */
intro:
intro: Exit status is the program's whole answer to the shell: 0 means it
intro: worked, 1 means it failed at its job, 2 means you called it wrong (a
intro: usage error). Errors and usage go to stderr; only results go to stdout.
intro:
intro: Reading standard input byte by byte is `getchar`, which returns the
intro: next byte or the constant EOF. EOF is a negative int (-1 on every libc
intro: here), outside any byte's range, so the variable must be an `int`.
intro: Store it in a `char` and it breaks: where char is signed (x86) a real
intro: 0xFF byte looks like EOF; where char is unsigned (ARM, a phone) EOF
intro: becomes 255 and the loop never ends:
intro:
intro:     int c;
intro:     while ((c = getchar()) != EOF)
intro:         count++;
intro:
intro: A line at a time is `fgets(buf, sizeof buf, stdin)`. It keeps the
intro: newline when the line fits, and returns NULL at end of input.
---
q: addup.c -- add the numbers you are given
spec: Add up the integer arguments and print the total and a newline.
spec:
spec: No arguments: print `usage: addup N...` to stderr, exit 2.
spec: An argument that is not a whole number (empty, `12abc`, too big for a
spec: `long`): print `addup: bad number: ARG` to stderr, exit 1, and print no
spec: total.
spec: A total that would overflow a `long`: print `addup: sum overflows` to
spec: stderr, exit 1. Check before you add -- overflowing a `long` is
spec: undefined behaviour.
file: addup.c
prog: #include <errno.h>
prog: #include <limits.h>
prog: #include <stdio.h>
prog: #include <stdlib.h>
prog: 
prog: int main(int argc, char **argv)
prog: {
prog: 	long sum = 0;
prog: 
prog: 	if (argc < 2) {
prog: 		fprintf(stderr, "usage: addup N...\n");
prog: 		return 2;
prog: 	}
prog: 	for (int i = 1; i < argc; i++) {
prog: 		char *end;
prog: 		long n;
prog: 
prog: 		errno = 0;
prog: 		n = strtol(argv[i], &end, 10);
prog: 		if (end == argv[i] || *end != '\0' || errno == ERANGE) {
prog: 			fprintf(stderr, "addup: bad number: %s\n", argv[i]);
prog: 			return 1;
prog: 		}
prog: 		if ((n > 0 && sum > LONG_MAX - n) || (n < 0 && sum < LONG_MIN - n)) {
prog: 			fprintf(stderr, "addup: sum overflows\n");
prog: 			return 1;
prog: 		}
prog: 		sum += n;
prog: 	}
prog: 	printf("%ld\n", sum);
prog: 	return 0;
prog: }
case: addup 3 4
case: addup -5 12 1
case: addup 2>&1; echo $?
case: addup 3 '' 2>&1; echo $?
case: addup 7 12abc 2>&1; echo $?
case: addup 99999999999999999999 2>&1; echo $?
case: addup 9223372036854775807 1 2>&1; echo $?
hint: strtol with an end pointer: bad if end never moved, if *end is not the terminating zero, or if errno says ERANGE
