q: tally.c -- count what comes in on stdin
spec: Read all of standard input with `getchar`. Print, with a newline after
spec: each:
spec:
spec: `lines N`
spec: `chars M`
spec:
spec: N is the number of newline characters; M is the number of bytes read.
spec: Empty input gives `lines 0` and `chars 0`. A last line with no trailing
spec: newline still counts its bytes but adds no line. Bytes of any value
spec: must count, including 0xFF.
file: tally.c
prog: #include <stdio.h>
prog: 
prog: int main(void)
prog: {
prog: 	unsigned long lines = 0;
prog: 	unsigned long chars = 0;
prog: 	int c;
prog: 
prog: 	while ((c = getchar()) != EOF) {
prog: 		chars++;
prog: 		if (c == '\n')
prog: 			lines++;
prog: 	}
prog: 	printf("lines %lu\n", lines);
prog: 	printf("chars %lu\n", chars);
prog: 	return 0;
prog: }
case: printf 'a\nbc\n' | tally
case: tally </dev/null
case: printf 'x\377y\n' | tally
case: printf 'no newline' | tally
hint: the variable getchar fills must be an int -- in a char, EOF is either confused with byte 0xFF or never seen at all
